(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-female-reproductive-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"Female Reproductive System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 4)","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"The Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts arise from the coelomic epithelium on the lateral aspect of the mesonephros at about which week of development?","choices":["6th week","2nd week","12th week","20th week"],"answer":0,"exp":"The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts arise from the coelomic epithelium on the lateral aspect of the mesonephros at about the 6th week of development. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The lower portion of the vagina develops from which embryonic structure rather than the Müllerian ducts?","choices":["The allantois","The mesonephric duct","The gubernaculum","The urogenital sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lower vagina develops from the urogenital sinus, distinct from the upper vagina, which forms from the fused Müllerian ducts. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"In male embryos, the Müllerian ducts regress due to the action of which hormone?","choices":["Testosterone from Leydig cells","Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH)","Estradiol from ovarian follicles","Progesterone from the corpus luteum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH), secreted by fetal Sertoli cells, causes regression of the Müllerian ducts in males; without it, the ducts persist and develop into the female organs. — Slides 16–17 (Müllerian system)"},{"q":"The posterior triangle of the perineum, containing the anus in both sexes, is also called the:","choices":["Femoral triangle","Urogenital triangle","Anal triangle","Cystohepatic triangle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior triangle of the perineum is the anal triangle, containing the anus in both sexes. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The mons pubis, a boundary landmark of the female perineum, lies immediately anterior to which structure?","choices":["The coccygeal tip","The pubic symphysis","The sacral base","The anal verge"],"answer":1,"exp":"The mons pubis is a fatty eminence of skin and subcutaneous tissue lying immediately anterior to the pubic symphysis. — Slides 8, 66 (Boundaries / External Genitalia)"},{"q":"The urogenital triangle in the female is distinguished from the male's in that it contains the:","choices":["Anus","Base of the penis and scrotum","Urethral and vaginal orifices","Prostate"],"answer":2,"exp":"In females, the urogenital (anterior) triangle contains the urethral and vaginal orifices, in place of the base of the penis and scrotum found in males. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The ovarian ligament attaches the ovary to which structure?","choices":["The subcutaneous labia majora","The lateral pelvic side wall","The superior margin of the uterus","The anterior sacral surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ovarian ligament attaches the ovary to the superior margin (lateral fundus) of the uterus. — Slide 18 (Supporting Ligaments)"},{"q":"The cardinal (transverse cervical) and uterosacral ligaments are described as thickenings of:","choices":["Dense hyaline cartilage tissue","Voluntary skeletal muscle fibers","Peritoneum without muscle fibers","Subserous fascia and smooth muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cardinal (transverse cervical) and uterosacral ligaments are thickenings of subserous fascia and smooth muscle that help support the uterus and cervix. — Slide 31 (Uterus support)"},{"q":"The uterosacral ligaments run from the inferior uterus to which structure?","choices":["The ischial tuberosity","The pubic symphysis","The labia majora","The sacrum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uterosacral ligaments extend from the inferior uterus (cervix) posteriorly to the sacrum. — Slide 36 (Support of the Uterus)"},{"q":"Which muscle group listed is part of the levator ani rather than a separate perineal muscle?","choices":["Puborectalis","Ischiocavernosus","Bulbospongiosus","Dartos"],"answer":0,"exp":"The puborectalis, along with the pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus, forms the levator ani muscle. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"The pubovaginalis, a muscle related to the vagina, is a part of which larger muscle?","choices":["Coccygeus","Levator ani","Rectus abdominis","Obturator internus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pubovaginalis is a component of the levator ani and is described among the muscles related to the vagina. — Slide 62 (Muscles Related to the Vagina)"},{"q":"Besides the pelvic diaphragm, the uterus is supported inferiorly by which additional muscular structure?","choices":["The urogenital diaphragm","The diaphragm (thoracic)","The transversus abdominis","The psoas major"],"answer":0,"exp":"Besides the pelvic diaphragm, the uterus is supported inferiorly by the urogenital diaphragm, part of the pelvic floor musculature. — Slide 36 (Support of the Uterus)"},{"q":"The blood supply to the uterus is provided by the uterine, ovarian, and which additional arteries?","choices":["Vaginal arteries","Renal arteries","Femoral arteries","Inferior epigastric arteries"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterus is supplied by the uterine and ovarian arteries along with the vaginal arteries. — Slide 35 (Uterine Layers — blood supply)"},{"q":"The vagina receives its blood supply from the uterine, vaginal, and which additional arteries?","choices":["Renal arteries","Pudendal arteries","Gonadal arteries only","Splenic arteries"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vagina receives blood from the uterine and vaginal arteries as well as the (internal) pudendal arteries. — Slide 59 (Vagina — blood supply)"},{"q":"The ovarian artery reaches the ovary by traveling within which ligament?","choices":["The ovarian ligament","The round ligament","The suspensory ligament","The uterosacral ligament"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ovarian artery travels within the suspensory ligament of the ovary before sending branches through the mesovarium to reach the ovary. — Slide 27 (Vasculature of Ovaries)"},{"q":"Superior lymphatic drainage of the vagina is to which nodes?","choices":["Popliteal fossa lymph nodes","Axillary lymph node chain","Internal and external iliac nodes","Submandibular lymph nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Superior lymphatic drainage of the vagina is to the internal and external iliac nodes, while the inferior vagina drains partly to the superficial inguinal nodes. — Slide 64 (Lymphatic Drainage of Vagina)"},{"q":"Lymphatic drainage of the ovaries follows the ovarian vessels to ascend to which nodes?","choices":["Lumbar (para-aortic) lymph nodes","Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","Axillary lymph nodes","Popliteal lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Ovarian lymphatics follow the ovarian blood vessels superiorly to ascend to the lumbar (para-aortic) lymph nodes. — Slide 27 (Lymphatics of Ovaries)"},{"q":"The uterine tube's lymphatic drainage is to which nodes?","choices":["Superficial inguinal nodes","Lumbar lymph nodes","Popliteal nodes","Submental nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The uterine tube's lymphatic drainage follows its ovarian/uterine vasculature to the lumbar lymph nodes. — Slide 48 (Uterine Tube)"},{"q":"The narrow, constricted region of the uterus located between the body and the cervix is the:","choices":["Infundibulum","Fundus","Fornix","Isthmus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The isthmus is the narrow, constricted region of the uterus located between the body and the cervix. — Slide 34 (Anatomic Regions)"},{"q":"The cervix communicates with the uterine body through the internal os and with the vagina through the:","choices":["External os","Infundibulum","Fornix","Hymen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cervical canal opens to the isthmus via the internal os and to the vagina via the external os. — Slides 33–34 (Regions of the Uterus)"},{"q":"The recess surrounding the vaginal portion of the cervix, used for surgical access and device placement, is the:","choices":["Infundibulum","Vestibule","Fornix","Ampulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fornix is the recess surrounding the vaginal portion of the cervix, used clinically for surgical access and device placement. — Slides 59–60 (Cervix and Vagina)"},{"q":"The long, wide portion of the uterine tube where fertilization typically occurs is the:","choices":["Intramural portion","Isthmus","Infundibulum","Ampulla"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ampulla is the long, wide portion of the uterine tube and the usual site of fertilization. — Slide 51 (Uterine Tube)"},{"q":"The hormone-sensitive inner lining of the uterus that cycles between puberty and menopause is the:","choices":["Serosa","Myometrium","Perimetrium","Endometrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The endometrium is the hormone-sensitive inner lining of the uterus, cycling in thickness between puberty and menopause. — Slides 35, 43 (Endometrium)"},{"q":"Which endometrial layer builds up each month in response to ovarian hormones and sloughs off at menstruation?","choices":["Stratum basalis layer","Stratum functionalis","Myometrium muscle wall","Perimetrium serosal covering"],"answer":1,"exp":"The stratum functionalis is the endometrial layer that builds up each month under ovarian hormone influence and sloughs off at menstruation. — Slide 43 (Endometrium)"},{"q":"The wall of the vagina consists of three tunics: an inner mucosa, a middle muscularis, and an outer:","choices":["Endometrium","Serosa","Perimetrium","Adventitia"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vaginal wall consists of an inner mucosa, a middle muscularis, and an outer adventitia. — Slide 56 (Vagina)"},{"q":"The labia minora are characterized histologically by a core of:","choices":["Dense hyaline cartilage with minimal vascular supply","Adipose tissue with coarse hair follicles and sweat glands","Skeletal muscle fibers arranged in dense bundles","Spongy connective tissue with erectile tissue and many small vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"The labia minora are characterized histologically by a core of spongy connective tissue containing erectile tissue and numerous small blood vessels, with no hair or fat. — Slide 74 (Labia Minora)"},{"q":"The labia majora are derived from the same embryonic structures as which male structure?","choices":["The scrotum","The penis","The prostate","The glans"],"answer":0,"exp":"The labia majora are derived from the same embryonic structures as, and are homologous to, the scrotum. — Slides 69, 73 (Labia Majora)"},{"q":"The posterior peritoneal recess between the uterus and the rectum is the:","choices":["Mesovarium","Vesicouterine pouch","Rectouterine pouch","Broad ligament"],"answer":2,"exp":"The rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) is the posterior peritoneal recess lying between the uterus and the rectum, the most dependent point of the peritoneal cavity in females. — Slide 12 (Anatomy of the Female Reproductive System)"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the uterus is classified as:","choices":["Subperitoneal","Retroperitoneal","Intraperitoneal","Extraperitoneal"],"answer":2,"exp":"With respect to the peritoneum, the uterus is classified as intraperitoneal, draped by the broad ligament. — Slide 31 (Uterus)"},{"q":"The broad ligament is best classified as which type of structure?","choices":["A dense fibrous cord","A fold of peritoneum","A smooth muscle band","A serous gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"The broad ligament is a fold of peritoneum that drapes over the uterus, uterine tubes, and ovaries, continuous with the perimetrium. — Slides 28, 39 (Broad ligament / perimetrium)"}]};
